# Line endings are load-bearing here, not cosmetic.
#
# Without this file, a checkout on a machine with `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for
# Windows default, and what WSL clones often inherit) rewrites every LF to CRLF --
# including the shell scripts. `sh` then chokes on the trailing \r with errors as
# unhelpful as `"elif" unexpected`, so `tv-hook.sh` silently stops reporting agent events
# and `install.sh` refuses to run at all.
#
# It also reaches production: .github/workflows/build.yml publishes `packaging/install.sh`
# to the GitHub Release straight from its checkout, so a CRLF conversion there ships a
# broken installer to every Linux and macOS user, and breaks `tintaview update` on those
# platforms with it. (Building on ubuntu-latest is the other half of that guarantee; this
# file is what keeps it true no matter which runner the job ever moves to.)
*.sh text eol=lf

# The other direction, for the one interpreter that genuinely wants CRLF: cmd.exe can
# mis-parse LF-only batch files. PowerShell is happy either way, but keeping .ps1
# alongside .cmd means the whole Windows-facing set is consistent.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never "fix" line endings inside binary assets. `.icns` is listed even though nothing
# generates one today — assets/source/ is where re-exported artwork lands, and a stray
# CRLF conversion there is silent corruption.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
